clockwise wrote:
> Does someone have a perl script that I could run over
> my logfile, to give me my number of unique deaths?

Keep in mind that the "uniqueness" of those deaths is as decided by the
tourney masters; the groupings may or may not make sense to you for
some things.  In particular, I understand patita's still somewhat
bitter that each rider's corpse isn't unique to itself ;-)

I used the following script to keep track of my unique deaths during
the tourney, and to check on other peoples' uniques as well.  You'll
need the death_yes.txt, death_no.txt, and an aadrvark-flavor scores
file to use this script.  The script doesn't win any points for
elegance, either, so fixing it up to run nicely and readably is left as
an exercise for the would-be maintainer ;-)

 -t

#!/usr/bin/perl

if ($#ARGV != 3) {
    print "Usage: $0 death_yes death_no results person\n";
    exit(-1);
}

$death_yes = $ARGV[0];
$death_no = $ARGV[1];
$resultFile = $ARGV[2];
$person = $ARGV[3];

open DEATH_YES, $death_yes or die "Failed to open death_yes $death_yes:
$!\n";
@allInclusions = <DEATH_YES>;
close DEATH_YES;
chomp(@allInclusions);
$numKnownUniques = scalar(@allInclusions);

open DEATH_NO, $death_no or die "Failed to open death_no $death_no:
$!\n";
@allExclusions = <DEATH_NO>;
close DEATH_NO;
chomp(@allExclusions);

open RESULTS, $resultFile or die "Failed to open results $resultFile:
$!\n";


@foundUnique = ();
@nonUnique = ();
@rulingPlease = ();
$allParsed = "";

%rejected = ();
for ($noIdx = 0; $noIdx <= $#allExclusions; $noIdx++) {
    $rejected{$allExclusions[$noIdx]} = 0;
}

while (<RESULTS>) {
    chomp($line = $_);
    @lineParts = split(/:/, $line);
    $score = $lineParts[1];
    $who = $lineParts[15];
    $death = $lineParts[16];
    if (1 || $who eq "name=$person") {
	$did_reject = 0;
	for ($noIdx = 0; $noIdx <= $#allExclusions; $noIdx++) {
	    if ($line =~ /death=$allExclusions[$noIdx]/) {
		$rejected{$allExclusions[$noIdx]}++;
		$did_reject = 1;
	    }
	}
	if (!$did_reject) {
	    $uniqued = 0;
	    for ($yesIdx = 0; !$uniqued && $yesIdx <= $#allInclusions;
$yesIdx++) {
		if ($death =~ /$allInclusions[$yesIdx]/) {
		    push (@foundUnique, $allInclusions[$yesIdx]);
		    splice(@allInclusions, $yesIdx, 1);
		    $uniqued = 1;
		}
	    }
	    if ($uniqued) {
		$allParsed .= "unique: $death\n";
	    }
	    if (!$uniqued) {
		$nonuniq = 0;
		for ($maybeIdx = 0; !$nonuniq && $maybeIdx <= $#foundUnique;
$maybeIdx++) {
		    if ($death =~ /$foundUnique[$maybeIdx]/) {
			$allParsed .= "NON-UNIQUE: $death\n";
			push (@nonUnique, $death);
			$nonuniq = 1;
		    }
		}
		if (!$nonuniq) {
		    $allParsed .= "NEEDS A RULING: $death\n";
		    push (@rulingPlease, $death);
		}
	    }
	}
    }
}

print $allParsed;

foreach $key (sort keys %rejected) {
    print "$key: $rejected{$key}\n";
}

print "Of the non-rejected, found " . scalar(@foundUnique) . " unique
deaths and " . scalar(@nonUnique) . " repeated deaths.\n";
if (scalar(@foundUnique)) {
    printf("That's %2.1f%% of the %d known uniques, and %2.1f%% unique
of all of %s's deaths, with %d left to categorize\n",
	   100 * scalar(@foundUnique) / $numKnownUniques, $numKnownUniques,
	   100 * scalar(@foundUnique) / (scalar(@foundUnique) +
scalar(@nonUnique)),
	   $person, scalar(@rulingPlease));
} else {
    print "Percentages don't really matter at this point, do they?\n";
}



